A reporting/speech introduction ending in a colon can be followed by a complete independently presented quoted utterance in a new paragraph. This is a permitted quotation convention, not an obligatory syntax error. Distinguish such an utterance or quotation block from a merely quoted term inside a larger unfinished grammatical construction.

After a completed quoted utterance and its completed surrounding reporting clause, a subsequent narration or new utterance may stand independently. A fragment-looking new opening does not demonstrate that a word or obligatory part was left unfinished on the left. Do not disguise missing OCR characters in a new narrative by appending it to a finished previous quotation.

Do not certify this exception for a word cut within an unfinished quotation, a quoted term that is only one part of an unfinished external clause, or an ancillary note misidentified as main text. Require the actual complete quotation/utterance convention and no compulsory unfinished left construction.

This is only a boundary exception check. It does not approve source ownership, authorize missing-text repair, or pardon another gap. If the legitimate quotation/narration boundary cannot be established, answer FALSE.

